; The four roundings plus `sign`, all pinned on negative and half-way
; inputs — the only inputs on which the four differ from each other.
;
; Form 5 is the one that decides a convention: (round 2.5) is 3, not 2.
; `round` is documented as "half away from zero", so it is C's `round`,
; not the banker's rounding that IEEE 754 makes the default for every
; other operation in this evaluator. An author summing rounded values gets
; a bias away from zero, on purpose. Form 3 is the same rule mirrored:
; (round -1.5) is -2, not -1.
;
; `sign` at exactly zero (form 8) is 0, not 1 — so `sign` is a three-way
; classifier, not a branchless x < 0 ? -1 : 1.
(abs -3.5)
(floor -1.5)
(ceil -1.5)
(round -1.5)
(round 1.5)
(round 2.5)
(sign -3.5)
(sign 2.0)
(sign 0.0)
